\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate how prompt schema size and inter-type confusability affect zero-shot LLM extraction across three information extraction tasks: named entity recognition (NER), relation extraction (RE), and event detection (ED).

\subsection{Tasks and Datasets}
\label{sec:datasets}

\paragraph{Few-NERD (NER).}
Few-NERD \cite{PLACEHOLDER_fewnerd} provides 66 fine-grained entity types organized into 8 coarse categories (person, location, organization, building, art, product, event, other). The dataset spans diverse domains, making it suitable for cross-domain evaluation. We sample 1{,}000 test instances and evaluate with token-level text matching.

\paragraph{FewRel (RE).}
FewRel \cite{PLACEHOLDER_fewrel} contains 80 relation types sourced from Wikidata. We combine the training and validation splits to obtain the full set of 80 relations and sample 1{,}000 balanced instances. Each instance provides a sentence with marked head and tail entities; the task is to classify the relation.

\paragraph{MAVEN (ED).}
MAVEN \cite{PLACEHOLDER_maven} defines 168 event types derived from the FrameNet ontology, making it a same-domain schema. We use the validation set (8{,}042 sentences containing 18{,}904 event mentions) for evaluation, as test set labels are not publicly available. The task requires both trigger identification and event type classification.

Table~\ref{tab:datasets} summarizes the dataset statistics, including the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) ranges defined in \S\ref{sec:scs}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrlll}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Task} & \textbf{\# Types} & \textbf{\# Samples} & \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{SCS Range} & \textbf{Embedding} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & NER & 66 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.77--0.93 & Qwen3-32B \\
FewRel & RE & 80 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.42--0.97 & MiniLM \\
MAVEN & ED & 168 & 8{,}042 & Same & 0.87--0.97 & MiniLM \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Dataset statistics. SCS Range indicates the min--max pairwise Schema Confusability Scores across all type pairs. Embedding column indicates the model used for SCS computation.}
\label{tab:datasets}
\end{table}

\subsection{Schema Confusability Score (SCS)}
\label{sec:scs}

We introduce the Schema Confusability Score to quantify semantic overlap between types in a schema. Given a type $t$ with name string $\text{name}(t)$, an embedding function $\text{embed}(\cdot)$ maps the type name to a vector representation. The \textbf{pairwise SCS} between types $t_i$ and $t_j$ is:
\[
\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \cos\bigl(\text{embed}(\text{name}(t_i)),\; \text{embed}(\text{name}(t_j))\bigr)
\]

For a batch of types $B = \{t_1, \ldots, t_k\}$, the \textbf{Batch Confusability Score (BCS)} aggregates all pairwise similarities:
\[
\text{BCS}(B) = \frac{1}{\binom{k}{2}} \sum_{i < j,\; t_i, t_j \in B} \text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)
\]

We compute SCS with two embedding models: (1)~Qwen3-32B \cite{PLACEHOLDER_qwen3} hidden-state representations, which capture the extraction model's own type representations, and (2)~all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{PLACEHOLDER_minilm}, a 384-dimensional general-purpose sentence encoder. Both use type name strings only, without definitions or descriptions, to reflect the information available to the model at inference time.

\subsection{Experimental Design}
\label{sec:exp_design}

We conduct two complementary experimental paradigms.

\paragraph{Experiment 1: Count Curves.}
We vary the number of types presented in the prompt schema to measure performance as a function of schema size. For each count level, we sample 5 random type batches and evaluate the same 1{,}000 test samples across all batches. At count${}=1$, every type is evaluated individually as a solo baseline. The count levels are: \{1, 2, 6, 12, 22, 44, 66\} for Few-NERD, \{1, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 80\} for FewRel, and \{1, 6, 12, 22, 44, 64\} for MAVEN.

\paragraph{Experiment 2: BCS-Controlled.}
We fix the type count and vary the Batch Confusability Score to isolate the effect of inter-type similarity from schema size. For each count level, we construct one low-BCS batch (types with the lowest pairwise similarities), one high-BCS batch (types with the highest pairwise similarities), and 25 random batches. We run this paradigm on Few-NERD at count$\,\in\{6, 12\}$ and on FewRel at count$\,\in\{8, 16\}$, the latter evaluated on both a 16-relation pool and the full 80-relation pool.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

We use task-appropriate metrics that reflect the distinct nature of each extraction problem.

\paragraph{NER (Few-NERD): Token-level F1.}
A predicted entity is a true positive if the predicted text span exactly matches (case-insensitive) a gold entity text \emph{and} the predicted type matches the gold type. We report Precision, Recall, and F1.

\paragraph{RE (FewRel): Accuracy.}
Each sample has exactly one ground-truth relation. A prediction is correct if the predicted relation label exactly matches the gold label. We report Accuracy.

\paragraph{ED (MAVEN): Relaxed F1.}
A predicted trigger is a true positive if (a)~any token overlap exists between the predicted trigger span and a gold trigger span, \emph{and} (b)~the predicted event type matches the gold event type. This relaxed matching is necessary because event triggers are inherently ambiguous in boundary (e.g., ``was killed'' vs.\ ``killed''). We report Relaxed F1.

The choice of different metrics across tasks reflects their structural differences: NER is span-level extraction where precision--recall trade-offs are informative; RE is a classification task where accuracy directly measures correctness; ED combines trigger identification with type classification, requiring relaxed span matching to avoid penalizing legitimate boundary variation.

\subsection{Model and Inference}
\label{sec:model}

\paragraph{Primary model.}
We use Qwen3-32B-FP8 \cite{PLACEHOLDER_qwen3} served via vLLM (v0.20.2) \cite{PLACEHOLDER_vllm} on a single NVIDIA A6000 GPU with \texttt{max\_model\_len=4096} and \texttt{gpu\_memory\_utilization=0.95}. The prompt provides a JSON-formatted schema listing all type names for the current batch; the model outputs structured JSON containing extracted entities, relations, or events. We use greedy decoding (temperature${}\,=\,0$) for reproducibility.

\paragraph{Statistical estimation.}
All key metrics are accompanied by 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals computed from 1{,}000 bootstrap resamples using the percentile method.

\paragraph{Secondary model.}
As a multi-model validation, we additionally evaluate LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct-FP8 on a subset of conditions; these results are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_multimodel}.

